  "query_preview": "..\nRest of Today through Friday ...\n\nPatchy fog will linger for portions of the forecast area through \nthis afternoon, though areas and widespread fog are diminishing. \nWhen encountering any remaining fog, prepare for visibility \nreductions as low as 1 mile.\n\nA weak upper wave has brought light showers to portions of\nsouthern Montana today. This activity will continue into the \nafternoon and move northeast. The chance for precipitation will \ndiminish through the afternoon across most of the forecast area. \nOne exception to this looks to be in and along the mountains and \nfoothills west of Billings early this evening as another weak wave\ncomes through. Though the chance for showers increases (up to a \n30 percent chance) during trick or treat time out west, very \nlittle if any accumulations are forecast. As for the rest of the \narea, expect a cool and damp evening with temperatures in the 40sF\nbetween 5PM and 9PM MDT.\n\nAn upper low currently situated off the coast of Washington is\nforecast to dig into the Western U.S. tonight into Sunday. With\nthis, our weather pattern will become more active by Sunday\nafternoon and last into the start of the week. Widespread\nprecipitation is forecast in the Northern Rockies with promising\naccumulation amounts. Shower activity is forecast to begin\nincreasing over southwest aspects of the Absaroka/Beartooths late\ntonight and early Sunday morning, before spreading across the\nplains through the afternoon. Expect precipitation to continue\ninto Monday afternoon, though it will diminish from west to east.\n\nFor precipitation accumulations Sunday through Monday, totals \nrange from 0.25\" to over an inch of rain with the greatest \naccumulations over southeast Montana and portions of northern \nWyoming. The latest probability for at least 0.5\" of rain is 40 to\n75% around and west of Billings (greatest near Billings and the \nmountains west of Billings. For locations west of Billings, the \nchance is 75 to 95%. The probability for at least 1 inch of \nprecipitation is 50 to 75% for portions of southeastern Montana \nand the Bighorn Mountains. For locations including and south-west \nof Rosebud County (also including Sheridan County), the \nprobability is 10-50% lowest west of Billings.\n\nThis does not look to be a very cold system, with high\ntemperatures forecast in the 40s and 50sF Sunday and Monday. More\nimportantly though, lower elevation snowfall will be hard to come\nby as Sunday night temperatures are forecast to remain above \nfreezing across the lower elevations. Snow levels will drop as low\nas 5,500 feet by Monday morning though. North and west aspects of\nthe Absaroka/Beartooths are forecast to collect up to 6 inches of\nsnow tonight through Monday, locally greater amounts are possible\nfor the higher elevations. As for the Bighorns, the latest \nforecast has 4 to 8 inches, with locally higher amounts possible.\n\nIn addition to all of the precipitation, a cold front is set to\ncome through the region from the northwest late Sunday. This will\nbring widespread windy conditions on Monday, especially over \nsoutheast Montana and northern Wyoming. For much of the area, \nexpect wind gusts in the 20s to 40 mph, while southeast MT and\nnorthern WY will see gusts in the 40s to low 50s mph. The \nprobability for gusts of 50 mph or greater along the Dakotas \nborder and for Sheridan County is 40 to 75 percent. The greatest \nchance for Sheridan County is shown to be closer to the Johnson \nCounty line. The addition of these winds to the Bighorn Mountains \nwill make conditions much more difficult for anyone with plans in \nthe high country.\n\nWinds will diminish through Monday evening, as ridging rebuilds \nover the Northern Rockies. This will result in drier conditions \nand temperatures in the 50s to low 60sF for much of the week. A\nfew isolated showers are possible in the mountains throughout the\nweek, as some weak waves ripple through.\n\nMatos",
